Subject: [PATCH] cron: make skip_memory opt-in rather than hardcoded

Upstream hardcodes skip_memory=True for cron-initiated agent runs, on the
reasoning that cron system prompts would corrupt user representations. The live
Backbone system deliberately wants the opposite: cron agents read and write
persistent memory (hybrid provider + MEMORY.md), gated behind an explicit
config flag.

Discovered on 2026-07-25 as an UNCOMMITTED modification in the running
checkout, which the Phase 0 audit missed -- see docs/OPERATIONS.md. Without it,
all 8 cron jobs run with no memory access. That failure is silent: no crash, no
failed probe, just subtly worse agents.

Enable with, in config.yaml:

    cron:
      memory_enabled: true

--- a/cron/scheduler.py
+++ b/cron/scheduler.py
@@ -2824,7 +2824,13 @@ def run_job(job: dict) -> tuple[bool, str, str, Optional[str]]:
             # Without a workdir, keep cwd context discovery disabled.
             skip_context_files=not bool(_job_workdir),
             load_soul_identity=True,
-            skip_memory=True,  # Cron system prompts would corrupt user representations
+            # Local patch: upstream hardcodes skip_memory=True here. Backbone
+            # wants cron agents to read/write persistent memory, so it is
+            # opt-in via `cron: memory_enabled: true` in config.yaml.
+            # A hermes update reverts this file -- reapply if cron runs start
+            # logging "Memory is not available".
+            skip_memory=not bool((_cfg.get("cron") or {}).get("memory_enabled", False)),
             platform="cron",
             session_id=_cron_session_id,
             session_db=_session_db,
